Subject: Training required from 5 October: moving to the LSV Comms view and new incident categories

Hi all,

We're making two changes to how we work in State Communications this season: all officers will move to the LSV Comms view, and we're updating how we categorise incidents. Both go live on Saturday 14 November, the start of the season.

Training runs from 5 October, and everyone taking shifts this season will need to complete it before go live. This email covers what's changing, the training dates and what we need from you, so please read it through and put the dates in your calendar.

THE CHANGES IN BRIEF

We'll cover the detail in the training sessions. Here is the short version.

1. Moving to the LSV Comms view
All officers will use the LSV Comms view this season. Maintaining two interfaces means building and testing everything twice. Consolidating onto one lets us put that effort into making a single system work really well for you.

The old dispatch interface won't be switched off overnight. It will stay available during the transition, and we plan to turn it off by 31 December. If you're still using it after go live, we'll get in touch to understand why. That's about learning what isn't working for you in the new view so we can fix it, not about catching anyone out.

2. Updating how we categorise incidents
We're updating our incident categories so they're more fit for purpose. This is foundational to how we grow as a service, and we'll walk through the reasoning and the new categories in the sessions.

Both changes have been through extensive consultation with EMCaR, volunteer representatives and SLSQ, and we want your feedback throughout the training.

THE TRANSITION PLAN

Training runs over six weeks in three two-week blocks, finishing as the season starts. The timing is deliberate. It's early enough that we're not changing things mid-season when it's busy, and late enough that you'll put the new skills to use straight away, rather than learning them in the off season and forgetting them before they're needed.

